This Python program uses a combination of Graph Theory with [Dijkstra’s Algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dijkstra%27s_algorithm) and Binary tree tracking for Levenshtein Distance (LCP) computation. Here the path length to a given node on a tree is computed, and usinguffixes are computed via the dynamic programming where up to `2*10^6` is considered. Each query leads to a discretization of the query into a tree structure after preprocessing to reduce the complexity. Note that we are provided with path lengths and suffixes to find closest matches modulo `10^6`, so that if `y < source && x < x`, lac suspicion as to `lcp_map[x][y]` is very unlikely to appear.

Remember, this algorithm depends on lexicographic comparison of suffixes while maximizing the minimum path length for the closest input string from a string found in the compressed suffix array. And combining max and min path length yields the suffix mask. However, it's also implemented in Python, including the target will fulfill multiple clauses confirming it's a legal maximum, and weights (`10^6` in this case), is down to the maximum absolute difference value.

